### Method reminder

A transition element is a d-block element that forms at least one stable ion with a **partly filled** d sub-shell. A d-block element whose every common ion has an empty ($d^0$) or full ($d^{10}$) d sub-shell is not a transition element.

### Solution

Examine the d sub-shell of a common ion of each element:

- titanium ($Z = 22$): $\mathrm{Ti^{3+}}$ is $[\mathrm{Ar}]\,3d^{1}$, partly filled;
- vanadium ($Z = 23$): $\mathrm{V^{3+}}$ is $[\mathrm{Ar}]\,3d^{2}$, partly filled;
- copper ($Z = 29$): the atom has a full 3d sub-shell, but the common ion $\mathrm{Cu^{2+}}$ is $[\mathrm{Ar}]\,3d^{9}$, partly filled, so copper **is** a transition element (the trap in this question);
- zinc ($Z = 30$): its only common ion, $\mathrm{Zn^{2+}}$, is $[\mathrm{Ar}]\,3d^{10}$, a full sub-shell. Zinc is a d-block element but not a transition element.

**Answer: d.**
